{
  "title": "Benché {csp:sia} tardi, ce la {fs:faremo}",
  "translation": "Although it's late, we'll make it",
  "topics": "Conjunctions with the congiuntivo, verbs with pronouns: farcela, andarsene, metterci, volerci",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Conjunctions that require the congiuntivo"
      },
      "These conjunctions introduce something that is conceded, a condition or a purpose. They are always followed by the *congiuntivo*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Idea",
            "Conjunctions",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Although",
              "benché, sebbene, nonostante",
              "Benché {csp:sia} tardi, {ip:esco}."
            ],
            [
              "On condition that",
              "purché, a patto che",
              "{ip:Vengo}, purché tu {csp:paghi}."
            ],
            [
              "So that",
              "affinché, perché",
              "Te lo {ip:dico} perché tu lo {csp:sappia}."
            ],
            [
              "Before",
              "prima che",
              "{imv:Parti} prima che {csp:piova}."
            ],
            [
              "Without",
              "senza che",
              "{pp:È uscito} senza che lo {csi:vedessi}."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "Compare the two uses of *perché*. *Because* takes the indicative: *{ip:Resto} perché {ip:piove}*. *{ip:So} that* takes the *congiuntivo*: *{ip:Parlo} piano perché tu {csp:capisca}*.",
      "*Anche se* (even if, although) takes the indicative, so it's the easy option in everyday speech: *Anche se {ip:è} tardi, {ip:esco}*.",
      {
        "h": "Same person? Use the infinitive"
      },
      "With *prima* and *senza*, when both parts have the same subject, use *di* or nothing with an infinitive: *{ip:Mangio} prima di uscire. {ip:Esco} senza salutare.*",
      {
        "h": "Verbs with a built-in pronoun"
      },
      "Some very common verbs have *ci*, *ne* or *la* fixed inside them, and the meaning changes completely.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "Meaning",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "farcela",
              "to manage, to make it",
              "Ce la {ip:faccio}. Non ce la {ip:faccio} più!"
            ],
            [
              "andarsene",
              "to go away",
              "Me ne {ip:vado}. Se n'{pp:è andato}."
            ],
            [
              "metterci",
              "to take (a person's time)",
              "Ci {ip:metto} due ore."
            ],
            [
              "volerci",
              "to take (time needed in general)",
              "Ci {ip:vuole} un'ora. Ci {ip:vogliono} due ore."
            ],
            [
              "avercela con",
              "to be angry with",
              "Ce l'{ip:ho} con te."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "The conjugated part changes as usual, and the small words are fixed: *ce la {ip:faccio}, ce la {ip:fai}, ce la {ip:fa}*; *me ne {ip:vado}, te ne {ip:vai}, se ne {ip:va}*.",
      "*Metterci* has a person as its subject: *Marco ci {ip:mette} un'ora*. *Volerci* is impersonal, and agrees with the amount of time: *ci {ip:vuole} un'ora, ci {ip:vogliono} due ore*."
    ]
  },
